sentinelT -- THE SENTINEL-AS-DATA PROMOTIONS, one section per site.

A sentinel-as-data site announces a failure and then hands its caller A VALUE
THAT LOOKS LIKE A SUCCESSFUL ANSWER -- `target`, `tempField`, `result`. The
caller cannot tell the refusal from a result, which is the whole defect: the
failure is reported to a human on stderr and concealed from the program.

Tony ruled these promoted one at a time, 2026-09-05, each with two rows:
THE CALLER'S READ OF THE NULL, and the F-41-style row that THE STATEMENT AFTER
DOES NOT RUN. Both are needed and neither implies the other -- a site could
start returning null while the block carried on, or could terminate while still
handing back a value.

    ST-1  opAddPointer   returned `target`
    ST-2  opRem          returned `tempField`, which at that point HAS NO DATA
    ST-3  THE STAR RULING -- `*x` on a field holding no group yields the
          testable nothing and does NOT refuse, so `if *x` takes its else arm
          and the activation carries on. Refusal is for CATEGORY errors; asking
          a field what it holds is a legitimate question with a legitimate empty
          answer. The CONSUMER of the null is what refuses, and it names the
          construct the writer actually typed.

⚠⚠ THE CALLER ROW IS 111 BECAUSE OF THE STORE RULING, AND IT READ ITS OWN TAG
BEFORE THAT RULING LANDED. The first cut of this fixture measured the target
BLANKED: a refused right-hand side is null, and opAssign's `else
target.clearData()` is what destroys the field. So the refusal was terminal for
the action AND destructive to its own target in the same breath.

Tony ruled it out the same day -- AN ARMED STATEMENT STORES NOTHING -- and the
row is now 111, unchanged.

⚠ AND WHERE THE CHECK LIVES IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. It was first put at
runOP's entry, which is the obvious place and does not work: AN ASSIGNMENT
DISPATCHES BEFORE ITS OWN RIGHT-HAND SIDE. A trace reads `op== refused=0` and
only then `op=+*`, with the refusal firing later still, inside the +* dispatch.
The enclosing operator is entered while the arm is clear, so an entry check can
never see its own operand refuse. The check belongs where the STORE is, in
opAssign.

⚠ THE TWO ROWS STILL PULL OPPOSITE WAYS, which is why both are kept: the caller
row says WHAT the refusal handed back, the after row says THAT the activation
stopped. A site returning null but carrying on would pass the first and fail the
second; one that stopped while still handing back `target` would show 111 here
and fail the first.

⚠ AND stRead's 111 IS A REAL STARTING VALUE, not a default. A row wanting 0
could be satisfied by a field that was never written at all; 111 can only be
there because nothing overwrote it.

⚠⚠ THE NULL OPERAND COMES FROM A SUBSCRIPT MISS, NOT FROM ANOTHER REFUSAL, AND
THAT IS DELIBERATE. incant/pointerT's F2 makes its null with `ptBagN +* *ptOther`
-- the STAR refuses and hands +* the null. That was fine while a refusal only
announced; now the star's refusal ARMS FIRST, so the block would stop on the
STAR and the row could not say which refusal did it. `Grokking["stNoSuchRule"]`
is a genuine null with no refusal in front of it, so ST-1 isolates opAddPointer.

ONE stop(), sentinel at the foot.
